---
const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Does MTA-STS require DNSSEC?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "No. MTA-STS is enforced through a DNS TXT record plus an HTTPS-hosted policy file with a valid certificate. DNSSEC is unrelated." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "What is the difference between testing and enforce mode?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Testing reports TLS failures without affecting delivery; enforce requires valid TLS and bouncing non-compliant senders. Start in testing, watch reports, then enforce." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "How long is the policy max_age?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "You set it in seconds; 86400 (one day) suits testing, and 604800 (a week) is common for stable enforce-mode policies." } }
  ],
};
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

---
<BaseLayout title="MTA-STS: Enforcing TLS for Inbound Email" description="MTA-STS explained: the DNS-published policy that forces TLS for incoming mail, how it differs from STARTTLS, and TLS-RPT reporting."  
 breadcrumb={[["Home", "/"], ["Email Authentication", "/email-authentication/"], ["MTA-STS"]]}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/email-authentication/" class="hover:text-accent">Email Authentication</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">MTA-STS</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">MTA-STS: Enforced TLS for Inbound Mail</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      STARTTLS upgrades an SMTP connection to TLS <em>if both sides agree</em>, which invites
      downgrade attacks: strip the handshake and mail flows in plaintext. MTA-STS closes that by
      publishing a DNS policy that says: my mail servers demand valid TLS, always.
    </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">MTA-STS policy modes</h2>
    <section class="not-prose my-4" aria-label="Reference table">
      <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="pdns-table" style="width:100%">
          <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">none</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">No policy published</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">testing</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Reports failures, no enforcement</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">enforce</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Bounces non-TLS delivery</td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>
<nav class="pd-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#how-it-works">How it works</a></li>
        <li><a href="#the-record">The record</a></li>
        <li><a href="#tls-rpt-the-reports-that-make-it-managea">TLS-RPT: the reports that make it manageable</a></li>
        <li><a href="#who-needs-it">Who needs it</a></li>
        <li><a href="#related">Related</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>
      <p>
        Two pieces. First, a TXT record at <code>_mta-sts.yourdomain.com</code> announcing that a
        policy exists and its version. Second, the policy itself served over HTTPS at
        <code>https://mta-sts.yourdomain.com/.well-known/mta-sts.txt</code>, listing your MX hosts
        and the mode: <code>testing</code> (report only) or <code>enforce</code> (refuse
        non-TLS delivery). Sending servers fetch the policy, verify the certificate, and only
        deliver over trustworthy TLS.
      </p>
      <h2 id="the-record">The record</h2>
      <pre class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-accent-soft">_mta-sts.example.com.  IN  TXT  "v=STSv1; id=2026082201"</pre>
      <p>
        The <code>id</code> changes to push policy updates. Keep a valid HTTPS cert on the policy
        host; an unreachable policy host weakens the whole mechanism.
      </p>
      <h2 id="tls-rpt-the-reports-that-make-it-managea">TLS-RPT: the reports that make it manageable</h2>
      <p>
        Its companion record (<code>_smtp._tls</code>) tells providers where to email delivery
        failures: which senders could not do TLS, which certificates failed. Roll out in testing
        mode with reports for a few weeks, then enforce. Our homepage scanner checks both records.
      </p>
      <h2 id="who-needs-it">Who needs it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Anyone receiving sensitive mail: finance, healthcare, legal.</li>
        <li>Compliance frameworks that name transport encryption.</li>
        <li>Organizations completing the full authentication stack: SPF, DKIM, <a href="/dmarc/">DMARC</a>, MTA-STS.</li>
      </ul>
      
<h2 id="related">Related</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/bimi/">BIMI</a></li>
        <li><a href="/email-authentication/">Email authentication hub</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Does MTA-STS require DNSSEC?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">No. MTA-STS is enforced through a DNS TXT record plus an HTTPS-hosted policy file with a valid certificate. DNSSEC is unrelated.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What is the difference between testing and enforce mode?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Testing reports TLS failures without affecting delivery; enforce requires valid TLS and bouncing non-compliant senders. Start in testing, watch reports, then enforce.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How long is the policy max_age?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">You set it in seconds; 86400 (one day) suits testing, and 604800 (a week) is common for stable enforce-mode policies.</p></details></div>
      </div>
</BaseLayout>
